      "reason": "Primary professional biography and company death announcement support the compact career."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S3",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>John Williams was an American property developer who founded Post Properties and co-founded Preferred Apartment Communities. Educated at Georgia Tech, he built a career in apartment development, ownership and management. He took Post public as a real estate investment trust and led Preferred as chairman and chief executive until his death.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "John Williams made landscaping a selling point for apartment communities, using tulips and other flowers to distinguish his developments. He began Post Properties without an office, conducting business from an IHOP restaurant. His approach to work included a weekly ritual: every Saturday, he wrote his goals and priorities on a yellow legal pad.\n\nHe also reconsidered decisions that had changed Atlanta. After buying and demolishing the mixed residential and shopping block at Pershing Point, he later described its destruction as his greatest professional regret. He concluded that restoring the existing apartments would have preserved the kind of neighbourhood his later developments sought to create.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 89,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Reported first-hand family recollections and the author's1998 interview support concrete working habits and a specific change of judgement."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S5",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 104,
    "html": "<p>John Williams made landscaping a selling point for apartment communities, using tulips and other flowers to distinguish his developments. He began Post Properties without an office, conducting business from an IHOP restaurant. His approach to work included a weekly ritual: every Saturday, he wrote his goals and priorities on a yellow legal pad.</p>\r\n<p>He also reconsidered decisions that had changed Atlanta. After buying and demolishing the mixed residential and shopping block at Pershing Point, he later described its destruction as his greatest professional regret. He concluded that restoring the existing apartments would have preserved the kind of neighbourhood his later developments sought to create.</p>\r\n"
  },
